  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Addressing Resource Limits**\n\nI\'ve hit a snag. The `generate_image` tool, powered by the Gemini API, reported a quota exceeded error. It looks like resource exhaustion is the bottleneck for the generation process, which needs immediate attention. I\'m focusing on how to best handle such errors in the future.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Asset Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on asset management. The image generation failure triggered a pivot. Instead of trying to force generation despite limits, I\'m focusing on strategies to prevent broken images. The `image-generation` skill offers key insights: prioritize CDN URLs or placeholder images. The recent user feedback (\"Where is the photo of the therapist and the sent images? They are not loading.\") has highlighted the issue. It\'s possible the images *are* available, but either not accessed correctly, not downloaded, or in a temporary location!\n\n\n**Uncovering File Upload Mechanism**\n\nI\'m now tracing the path of user-uploaded images. My focus has shifted to understanding how files from the AI Studio chat interface are accessed. Initial searches for physical files yielded nothing, but I\'ve realized the crucial distinction: files aren\'t necessarily *on disk* in the container. Instead, the AI Studio preview environment utilizes a proxy or middleware mechanism, such as `/input_file_0.png`, to make these chat-uploaded files available at runtime. Now I need to explore how to access images provided by the user in this way!\n\n\n**Analyzing Request Interception**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. I realized the user\'s images aren\'t loading because Vite, our development server, doesn\'t handle requests for `/input_file_0.png`. I found that Vite, acting as a pure client-side SPA in development, lacks a `public` directory to serve these chat attachments directly. This means the image requests are resulting in 404 errors. I\'m now exploring how the platform should manage these attachments in the development environment.\n\n\n**Exploring File Handling**\n\nI\'m now investigating how to handle the `/input_file_*.png` requests. My working theory is that AI Studio\'s reverse proxy intercepts these requests, but it might depend on server health or the exact file naming. I\'m checking if there\'s any file references within the workspace, especially `/index.html`. Alternatively, I\'m considering using high-quality Unsplash photos as elegant fallbacks, which addresses user experience and error handling. I\'m also confirming what images the user submitted.\n\n\n**Uncovering Data Persistence**\n\nI\'m now grappling with the ephemeral nature of these chat-uploaded images. I\'ve confirmed that the model *can* access image references like `/input_file_0.png` within the conversation history, but they don\'t seem to persist on disk within the container. The key problem remains: the user\'s preview iframe can\'t display these images unless the web app can serve them. My current line of thought is whether there\'s a mechanism to write these \"virtual\" files to disk or fetch them from some other persistent location. I\'m exploring the nuances of conversation history and file access in the AI Studio environment.\n\n\n**Implementing Placeholder Images**\n\nI\'m now pivoting to image placeholders. Based on the `image-generation` skill and user feedback, I\'m focusing on fallback strategies. I\'ve realized Unsplash offers a great solution: elegant, high-quality images. I\'ve found an Unsplash URL for a male therapist in a suit: `https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1560250097-0b93528c311a?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=600&h=750`. This will provide a professional and appealing fallback when user images aren\'t available.\n\n\n**Refining Image Selection**\n\nI\'m now refining the image choices. The previous Unsplash image for a male therapist was a good starting point, but I\'ve located a better, more suitable portrait: `https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507003211169-0a1dd7228f2d?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=600&h=750`. I\'m also finding one for an avatar portrait and logo, with an eye on SVG or inline gold vector art.\n\n\n**Reverting Image Replacement**\n\nI\'m now reversing an unwanted change. The previous model replaced an elegant CSS logo with a broken image tag, resulting in 404 errors. I\'m focusing on restoring the original CSS logo, which uses a minimalist gold character and styling, for a seamless user experience. The file locations were `Navigation.tsx`, `Footer.tsx`, and `Hero.tsx`.\n\n\n**Confirming Proxy Interaction**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the user uploads are handled. I\'m focusing on the interaction between the chat attachments and the application. I am confirming if there\'s any proxy or alternative mechanism for transferring the user\'s uploaded images to the application, or whether we must rely entirely on external resources such as Unsplash. I\'m trying to ensure the user can view the uploaded images, or elegantly fall back to the placeholder images from Unsplash.\n\n\n"
